<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cancho Roano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cancho Roano is an archaeological site near Zalamea de la Serena in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its building, dating from about the 6th to the late 5th century BCE, is interpreted as a palace-sanctuary of the late Tartessian world and was deliberately burned and buried when it was abandoned. Finds of Greek pottery, jewellery and weapons show the links of inland elites with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cancho-roano.jpg" alt="Cancho Roano" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Axel Cotón Gutiérrez · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centro_de_Interpretaci%C3%B3n_del_Yacimiento_de_Cancho_Roano._Paneles_informativos_I_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cancho Roano, sometimes written Cancho Ruano, lies three miles from Zalamea de la Serena on the road towards Quintana de la Serena, in a small valley beside the stream Cagancha. It is the best preserved Tartessian site. The main body of the building is square and faces east, and it was surrounded by a deep moat that was kept permanently filled with water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site goes back to at least the 6th century BCE, and the dating of the objects found there puts its beginning at about 550 BCE. The building was later expanded and modified. At its end it appears to have been ritually burned and sealed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammed-earth">rammed earth</a>, in a manner similar to Etruscan practice. Its exact purpose is unknown. The altars make its religious character certain, while its defences suggest it may also have been a palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Excavations directed by John Maluquer de Motes began in 1978 and continued until 2001, uncovering the building with its moat, its many rooms and its altars. The site was declared a National Monument in 1986 and is open to the public, with an interpretation centre.</p><p>Interpretations of the building differ. Its sacred character and many small rooms have suggested to some a temple of sacred prostitution, perhaps dedicated to the Phoenician goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, an idea supported by looms found in two of the rooms, which recall the weavers of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a> in the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Similar rites have been seen in the imagery of other Phoenician sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, such as Gadir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castulo">Castulo</a> and La Quéjola. Another view sees a palace housing a harem whose members took part in the temple's rites.</p><p>Richard Freund proposed that Cancho Roano was a memorial city standing for the lost city of Tartessos, which he identified with Atlantis, reading concentric circles on a stele from the site as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Atlantis. Scholars have widely dismissed the theory, and the sign is regarded as a typical warrior's shield of the kind engraved on the Southwestern Stelae of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cancho Roano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeology shows an elite culture that adopted eastern goods and styles, seen in the gold of the El Carambolo treasure and the palace-sanctuary of <b>Cancho Roano</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tartessos">Tartessos</a></li></ul></section>
